BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Welcome II the Terrordome with Ashley Clark and Ngozi Onwurah
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260330
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260331
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be232-b8d5-82fb-2cc1-d5b4c041dab8@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Restoration world premiere\n\nMonday\, March 30\, 2026\n\nLoca
 tion: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1995 | 95 mi
 n. | UK | Color | English | Rated R | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Ngozi On
 wurah  \nWITH: Suzette Llewellyn\, Saffron Burrows\, Felix Joseph\, Valen
 tine Nonyela\n\nType: Screenings\n\nWith her 1995 feature Welcome II the T
 errordome\, Ngozi Onwurah became the first Black British female director t
 o have a film released theatrically in the UK. In a future ghetto riddled 
 with drugs and ruled by gangs\, a grieving mother’s anger ignites a viol
 ent uprising. Featuring a hip-hop score that functions as a Greek chorus\,
  this uncompromising film addresses the legacy of slavery and ongoing terr
 or of police and state violence. Named after a blistering 1990 track by Pu
 blic Enemy\, the film\, also written by Onwurah\, stands out not only for 
 its vividly disturbing content but also for its uniqueness in the predomin
 antly white and male landscapes of cyberpunk and British cinema. \nNote by
  Ashley Clark\, adapted from his essay in the Museum’s catalog Cyberpunk
 : Envisioning Possible Futures Through Cinema (2024).  \nClark and direct
 or Onwurah join us in conversation about this singular work.\nIn a separat
 ely ticketed event\, Clark also signs copies of his new book\, The World o
 f Black Film: A Journey Through Cinematic Blackness in 100 Films (Laurence
  King\, 2026). Copies will be available to purchase in advance of the prog
 ram.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/welcome-ii-the-terror
 dome-with-ashley-clark-019be232-b8d5-82fb-2cc1-d5b4c041dab8
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Book Signing: The World of Black Film with author Ashley Clark
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260330
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260331
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019cbf42-bca0-5425-247f-b38ae618f357@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Book Signings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, March 30\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nType: Book Signings\n\nAbout the authorAshley Clark is a writ
 er\, broadcaster\, and film programmer. He has organized numerous film sea
 sons at international venues including London's BFI Southbank\, New York's
  Museum of Modern Art\, the Brooklyn Academy of Music\, and Toronto's TIFF
  Lightbox. His writing has appeared in The Guardian\, Reverse Shot\, Sight
  & Sound\, and Film Comment\, and he is the author of Facing Blackness: Me
 dia and Minstrelsy in Spike Lee's Bamboozled (2015). Ashley was born in Lo
 ndon\, lives in Jersey City\, and works in New York\, where he has been th
 e curatorial director of the Criterion Collection since 2020.\nAbout the b
 ookThe World of Black Film: A Journey Through Cinematic Blackness in 100 F
 ilms is an entertaining\, informed\, and thought-provoking survey of influ
 ential Black films from around the globe. Starting with the unfinished sil
 ent comedy Lime Kiln Club Field Day (1913) and concluding with Steve McQue
 en's World War II epic Blitz (2024)\, this book takes readers on an exciti
 ng journey through an eclectic mix of classics and hidden gems spanning mo
 re than 100 years and 30 countries.\nBeautifully designed and bursting wit
 h eye-catching film imagery and poster art\, this is essential reading for
  general film fans\, enthusiasts of Black cinema\, educators\, and student
 s alike.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/book-signing-the-worl
 d-of-black-film-with-author-ashley-clark-019cbf42-bca0-5425-247f-b38ae618f
 357
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Psycho
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260330
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260331
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be236-4fe2-eaa6-db24-ca2586dcc299@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, March 30\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 49m\n\n1960 | 109 min. | USA | Black-and-White | 
 English | Rated R \nDIRECTED BY: Alfred Hitchcock  \nWRITTEN BY: Joseph S
 tefano\nWITH: Anthony Perkins\, Vera Miles\, John Gavin\, Janet Leigh\n\nT
 ype: Screenings\n\n“When I was a kid\,” director Bong Joon Ho writes i
 n the catalog for his current exhibition at the Academy Museum\, “I watc
 hed so many Alfred Hitchcock movies on Korean television. Hitchcock is sti
 ll one of my biggest inspirations. I’ve seen Psycho more than 50 times.
 ” This psychological thriller opens with Marion Crane (Janet Leigh) absc
 onding with $40\,000 cash from her employer in Phoenix to deliver to her d
 ebt-ridden fiancé in California. Along the way she stops at the Bates Mot
 el\, where an encounter with curious innkeeper Norman (Anthony Perkins) be
 gets one of the most harrowing and famous scenes in cinema history. An Ita
 lian poster from Bong’s personal collection hangs in the exhibition\, on
  view on Level 2\, and he included the title on his 2022 Sight & Sound lis
 t as one of 10 films that he considers the greatest of all time.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/psycho-019be236-4fe2-
 eaa6-db24-ca2586dcc299
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Psycho
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260401T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260401T163000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be235-cf1e-0716-d392-c7fcfdb310bd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, April 1\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mus
 eum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 49m\n\n1960 | 109 min. | USA | Black
 -and-White | English | Rated R \nDIRECTED BY: Alfred Hitchcock  \nWRITTEN
  BY: Joseph Stefano\nWITH: Anthony Perkins\, Vera Miles\, John Gavin\, Jan
 et Leigh\n\nType: Screenings\n\n“When I was a kid\,” director Bong Joo
 n Ho writes in the catalog for his current exhibition at the Academy Museu
 m\, “I watched so many Alfred Hitchcock movies on Korean television. Hit
 chcock is still one of my biggest inspirations. I’ve seen Psycho more th
 an 50 times.” This psychological thriller opens with Marion Crane (Janet
  Leigh) absconding with $40\,000 cash from her employer in Phoenix to deli
 ver to her debt-ridden fiancé in California. Along the way she stops at t
 he Bates Motel\, where an encounter with curious innkeeper Norman (Anthony
  Perkins) begets one of the most harrowing and famous scenes in cinema his
 tory. An Italian poster from Bong’s personal collection hangs in the exh
 ibition\, on view on Level 2\, and he included the title on his 2022 Sight
  & Sound list as one of 10 films that he considers the greatest of all tim
 e.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/psycho-019be235-cf1e-
 0716-d392-c7fcfdb310bd
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:My Fair Lady in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260401T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260401T223000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be20e-8af2-702b-afe5-7e7845b8cb41@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Academy Governor Gerald Quist \nSelected by the Mak
 eup Artists and Hairstylists Branch\n\nWednesday\, April 1\, 2026 at 7:30 
 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 50m\n\n19
 64 | 170 min. | USA | Color | Scope | English | Rated G | 4K DCP \nDIRECTE
 D BY: George Cukor \nWRITTEN BY: Alan Jay Lerner \nWITH: Audrey Hepburn\, 
 Rex Harrison\, Stanley Holloway\, Theodore Bikel\n\nType: Screenings\n\nTh
 is lavish film version of the hit Broadway musical from composer Frederick
  Loewe and lyricist/book writer Alan Jay Lerner—based on George Bernard 
 Shaw’s classic play Pygmalion—earned eight Oscars\, including Best Pic
 ture. Rex Harrison reprised his Tony Award-winning performance as the iras
 cible Professor Henry Higgins for the big screen and earned the Best Actor
  award. Audrey Hepburn is her typically enchanting self as his pupil Eliza
  Doolittle (singing voice provided by Marni Nixon)\, her transformation ai
 ded by makeup supervisor Gordon Bau and supervising hair stylist Jean Burt
  Reilly.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/my-fair-lady-in-4-k-0
 19be20e-8af2-702b-afe5-7e7845b8cb41
END:VEVENT
