BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ikiru (To Live) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260403T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260403T203000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c062a-f565-75f5-4be0-16278de57735@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, April 3\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 23m\n\n1952 | 143 min. | Japan | Black-
 and-White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED 
 BY: Akira Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Shinobu Hashimoto\, Hide
 o Oguni \nWITH: Takashi Shimura\, Nobuo Kaneko\, Shin’ichi Himori\, Haru
 o Tanaka\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAkira Kurosawa explores embracing life thr
 ough death in aging bureaucrat Kanji Watanabe (Takashi Shimura)\, who spen
 ds his days on autopilot at his monotonous desk job. When Kanji discovers 
 that he has terminal stomach cancer\, he questions the meaning of life\, s
 earching for a reason to continue living with the little time he has left.
  Shimura’s quietly expressive and heartbreaking portrayal of Watanabe is
  widely considered a career-defining role for the actor. The film received
  the Special Prize of the Senate of Berlin at the 1954 Berlin Internationa
 l Film Festival.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/ikiru-to-live-in-35-m
 m-019c062a-f565-75f5-4be0-16278de57735
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Lupin the 3rd: The Castle of Cagliostro
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260404T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260404T121500
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be21e-9c42-b967-5e8b-23fcb32e8be6@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 4\, 2026 at 11 am\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 50m\n\n1979 | 110 min. | Japan | Color 
 | English (Dubbed) | Rated PG | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Hayao Miyazaki \nWRITTE
 N BY: Hayao Miyazaki\, Haruya Yamazaki \nWITH: Bob Bergen\, Joan Carol O
 ’Connell\, Michael McConnohie\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHayao Miyazaki’s 
 debut feature revisits a character he directed for television―gentleman 
 thief Lupin the 3rd. Grandson to Maurice Leblanc’s French literary maste
 r thief Arsène Lupin\, Lupin the 3rd is a Swinging Sixties-style\, James 
 Bond-like charmer who steals for thrills. Stuck with counterfeit bills aft
 er a Monaco casino heist\, Lupin and his sidekicks hightail it to the myst
 erious principality of Cagliostro to find the source of the fake money. In
  the process\, the trio becomes embroiled in palace intrigue between Count
  Cagliostro and Princess Clarisse. An energetic caper\, Lupin the 3rd: The
  Castle of Cagliostro is a stylish start to a brilliant oeuvre.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/lupin-the-3rd-the-cas
 tle-of-cagliostro-019be21e-9c42-b967-5e8b-23fcb32e8be6
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Follow That Bird with director Ken Kwapis
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260404T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260404T170000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be230-8d08-76b5-4f40-740527381e8e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: director Ken Kwapis and Debra Spinney\n\nSaturday\,
  April 4\, 2026 at 3 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\n
 Runtime: 1h 28m\n\n1985 | 88 min. | USA | Color | English\, American Sign 
 Language | Rated G | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Ken Kwapis  \nWRITTEN BY: Tony Ge
 iss\, Judy Freudberg  \nWITH: Carroll Spinney\, Jim Henson\, Frank Oz\, R
 ichard Hunt\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSince its debut on National Education T
 elevision in 1969\, Sesame Street has raised generations of children with 
 its playful combination of pure entertainment and early education segments
 \, achieved through a mix of animation\, live actors\, and skillful puppet
 ry. The first feature-length theatrical film to bring this small-screen co
 mmunity to the big screen\, Follow That Bird is a freewheeling road musica
 l that follows 8-foot-tall avian Big Bird (Carroll Spinney in a heart-melt
 ing performance) from his placement with a foster family to his trek back 
 to his real home on Sesame Street. \nWith delightful guest appearances by 
 Paul Bartel\, Sandra Bernhard\, John Candy\, Chevy Chase\, and Waylon Jenn
 ings\, along with show regulars Oscar the Grouch\, Mr. Snuffleupagus\, Gro
 ver\, Bert and Ernie\, and Elmo\, this seminal children’s film about fam
 ily\, belonging\, and growth has something for kids and adults alike.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/follow-that-bird-019b
 e230-8d08-76b5-4f40-740527381e8e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Throne of Blood (Kumonosu-Jo) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260404T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260404T210000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c062c-d5da-4ef3-d46b-0de4f02a5d56@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 4\, 2026 at 7 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 49m\n\nDIRECTED BY: Akira Kurosawa  \nW
 RITTEN BY: Hideo Oguni\, Shinobu Hashimoto\, Ryuzo Kikushima\, Akira Kuros
 awa  \nWITH: Toshiro Mifune\, Isuzu Yamada\, Takashi Shimura\, Akira Kubo
   \n1957 | 109 min. | Japan | Black-and-White | Japanese with English sub
 titles | Not Rated | 35mm\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAn adaptation of Shakespe
 are’s Macbeth\, this jidai-geki\, or period drama\, set in feudal Japan\
 , is part of a loose trilogy with The Lower Depths (1957\, screening Sunda
 y\, April 19) and The Hidden Fortress (1958\, screening Thursday\, April 2
 3). Shakespeare’s plays were translated for Japanese readers as early as
  1868\, though they were banned during World War II for not being original
  Japanese works\; Kurosawa wanted to take on the challenge of adapting a B
 ritish story to a Japanese sensibility. With staging based in traditional 
 Noh drama and visual design inspired by Musha-e warrior paintings\, Kurosa
 wa created what he considered one of his most experimental works.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/throne-of-blood-kumon
 osu-jo-in-35-mm-019c062c-d5da-4ef3-d46b-0de4f02a5d56
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:To Kill a Mockingbird in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260405T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260405T160000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be225-49b8-b538-9779-b453313a9b2e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Cecilia Peck\n\nSunday\, April 5\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\n
 Location: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 10m\n\n1962 | 1
 30 min. | USA | B&W | English | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Robert Mul
 ligan \nWRITTEN BY: Horton Foote \nWITH: Gregory Peck\, Brock Peters\, Mar
 y Badham\, Phillip Alford\n\nType: Screenings\n\nGregory Peck won the Best
  Actor Oscar for his warm and restrained performance as attorney Atticus F
 inch in director Robert Mulligan’s film version of Harper Lee’s classi
 c novel about racial injustice and family life in small-town Alabama. The 
 story is told from the perspective of Finch’s two children\, Jem and Sco
 ut\, and Mulligan and uncredited casting director\, Boaty Boatwright\, fou
 nd two talented unknowns for the parts\, Phillip Alford and Mary Badham\, 
 with Robert Duvall making an equally impressive film debut as the mysterio
 us Boo Radley. \nThe film also won for Horton Foote’s screenplay and its
  black-and-white art direction\, and was nominated for Best Picture\, Mull
 igan’s directing\, Elmer Bernstein’s beautiful score\, Russell Harlan
 ’s black-and-white cinematography\, and Badham’s effortlessly realisti
 c performance.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/to-kill-a-mockingbird
 -019be225-49b8-b538-9779-b453313a9b2e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Birthday Party in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260405T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260405T160000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0631-f17f-75d2-8873-0ea7c06383f4@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 5\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 3m\n\n1968 | 123 min. | UK | Color | En
 glish | Rated G | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: William Friedkin \nWRITTEN BY: Harol
 d Pinter \nWITH: Robert Shaw\, Patrick Magee\, Sydney Tafler\, Dandy Nicho
 ls\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHarold Pinter adapted his own 1957 play for this
  tense psychological drama\, the second film directed by Oscar winner Will
 iam Friedkin (The French Connection\, 1971). Robert Shaw plays Stanley\, a
  boarding-house resident\, whose quiet life is upended by the arrival of t
 wo sinister strangers. This faithful version of the stage classic\, Shaw
 ’s second appearance in a Pinter adaptation following 1964’s The Caret
 aker\, gave the star a rare chance to play a figure who is as much menaced
  as menacing\, and allowed moviegoers to experience the subtlety and range
  of his stage performances.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-birthday-party-in
 -35-mm-019c0631-f17f-75d2-8873-0ea7c06383f4
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sanshiro Sugata with Sanshiro Sugata Part II
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260405T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260405T213000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0632-e620-0e6b-b515-883c7db9c068@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Kerim Yasar\, associate professor of East Asian lan
 guages and cultures\n\nSunday\, April 5\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Aca
 demy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 19m\n\n1943 | 79 min. | Japa
 n | Black-and-White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm \
 nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa  \nWITH: Denjiro Okochi\, Susumu Fuj
 ita\, Yukiko Todoroki\, Ryunosuke Tsukigata \nPrint courtesy of The Japan 
 Foundation\, Los Angeles.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSanshiro Sugata in 35mmKu
 rosawa’s directorial debut\, adapted by the filmmaker from a book by Tsu
 neo Tomita\, deftly sets the stage for an unparalleled five-decade career.
  Set in 1882\, Sanshiro Sugata follows an aspiring martial artist determin
 ed to prove his worth under an impressive sensei. Strong but undisciplined
 \, Sugata gets into scraps that sow distrust and fear in his master and co
 mmunity. Tomita’s book established a story steeped in themes of honor an
 d masculinity\, and Kurosawa fleshed it out visually with organic\, fluid 
 camerawork\; staging that emphasizes depth of field\; and strong symbolism
  expressed through the power of nature and its untamable forces.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/sanshiro-sugata-with-
 sanshiro-sugata-part-ii-019c0632-e620-0e6b-b515-883c7db9c068
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Housemaid
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260406T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260406T163000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be236-c0f5-a6c3-91b6-44cee3f387d6@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, April 6\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 48m\n\n1960 | 108 min. | South Korea | 
 Black-and-White | Korean with English subtitles | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN B
 Y: Kim Ki-young \nWITH: Kim Jin-kyu\, Ju Jeung-nyeo\, Lee Eun-shim\, Um Ae
 ng-ran \nThe Housemaid was restored in 2008 by the Korean Film Archive (KO
 FA)\, in association with The Film Foundation’s World Cinema Project and
  HFR-Digital Film laboratory. Additional restoration funded by Armani\, Ca
 rtier\, Qatar Airways\, and Qatar Museum Authority.\n\nType: Screenings\n\
 nRadical and uncompromising\, Kim Ki-young’s Housemaid trilogy starts in
  1960 with a first chapter that continues to influence new generations of 
 filmmakers more than 60 years later. A deep survey on desire\, obsession\,
  and ego-driven revenge\, this domestic horror film follows a bourgeois fa
 mily who hires Myung-sook (Lee Eun-shim)\, an unsophisticated young woman\
 , as their housemaid. When she becomes smitten with patriarch Dong-sik Kim
 \, a series of strange episodes unfold\, and Myung-sook’s catastrophic i
 nfatuation threatens the family bond. Kim’s unconventional language of c
 inema depicts a bleak reality that is borderline ludicrous\, in an unconve
 ntional blend of genres that redefined filmmaking in Korea.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-housemaid-019be23
 6-c0f5-a6c3-91b6-44cee3f387d6
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Housemaid
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260408T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260408T163000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be236-e67e-aff1-6184-a875ef0a3ab9@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, April 8\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mus
 eum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 48m\n\n1960 | 108 min. | South Korea
  | Black-and-White | Korean with English subtitles | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTE
 N BY: Kim Ki-young \nWITH: Kim Jin-kyu\, Ju Jeung-nyeo\, Lee Eun-shim\, Um
  Aeng-ran \nThe Housemaid was restored in 2008 by the Korean Film Archive 
 (KOFA)\, in association with The Film Foundation’s World Cinema Project 
 and HFR-Digital Film laboratory. Additional restoration funded by Armani\,
  Cartier\, Qatar Airways\, and Qatar Museum Authority.\n\nType: Screenings
 \n\nRadical and uncompromising\, Kim Ki-young’s Housemaid trilogy starts
  in 1960 with a first chapter that continues to influence new generations 
 of filmmakers more than 60 years later. A deep survey on desire\, obsessio
 n\, and ego-driven revenge\, this domestic horror film follows a bourgeois
  family who hires Myung-sook (Lee Eun-shim)\, an unsophisticated young wom
 an\, as their housemaid. When she becomes smitten with patriarch Dong-sik 
 Kim\, a series of strange episodes unfold\, and Myung-sook’s catastrophi
 c infatuation threatens the family bond. Kim’s unconventional language o
 f cinema depicts a bleak reality that is borderline ludicrous\, in an unco
 nventional blend of genres that redefined filmmaking in Korea.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-housemaid-019be23
 6-e67e-aff1-6184-a875ef0a3ab9
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fight Club in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260408T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260408T223000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be20e-e790-8fb0-537b-2b697e1c3299@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: entertainment marketing executive Steve Siskind and
  Academy Governor David Dinerstein\nSelected by the Marketing and Public R
 elations Branch\n\nWednesday\, April 8\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Acad
 emy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 19m\n\n1999 | 139 min. | USA 
 | Color | Scope | English | Rated R | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: David Fincher \
 nWRITTEN BY: Jim Uhls \nWITH: Brad Pitt\, Edward Norton\, Helena Bonham Ca
 rter\, Meat Loaf Aday\n\nType: Screenings\n\nChuck Palahniuk’s bold nove
 l inspired this equally daring film version from director David Fincher\, 
 whose choices courted controversy before the work eventually earned cult-f
 avorite status. Edward Norton plays the unnamed hero\, who chafes against 
 the Ikea-furnished comfort of his office-drone life only to have his world
  turned upside down by a chance encounter with the anarchic Tyler Durden (
 Brad Pitt). Soon the fast friends have started “Fight Club\,” an under
 ground enterprise giving suppressed men the chance to unleash their violen
 t instincts. The film’s exemplary technical aspects include the Oscar-no
 minated sound effects editing of Ren Klyce and Richard Hymns.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/fight-club-019be20e-e
 790-8fb0-537b-2b697e1c3299
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Drunken Angel (Yoidore Tenshi) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260408T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260408T203000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0633-db8a-4cd0-8734-8871f33ced20@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, April 8\, 2026 at 8 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 38m\n\n1948 | 98 min. | Japan | Black-a
 nd-White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm. \nDIRECTED 
 BY: Akira Kurosawa  \nWRITTEN BY: Keinosuke Uekusa\, Akira Kurosawa \nWI
 TH: Takashi Shimura\, Toshiro Mifune\, Reizaburo Yamamoto\, Michiyo Kogure
 \n\nType: Screenings\n\n“In this picture I finally discovered myself\,
 ” Kurosawa said in 1960 of his Occupation-period drama about a consumpti
 ve gangster (Toshiro Mifune\, in a career-defining role) and the kind-hear
 ted\, alcoholic doctor (Takashi Shimura of Ikiru\, 1952) who treats him. S
 et in war-ravaged Tokyo and using real locations to ground the story in au
 thenticity\, critics of the day noted a journalistic quality in the film\,
  despite the director’s earlier The Most Beautiful (1944\, screening on 
 Saturday\, Apr 11) also exhibiting topical themes. Widely considered one o
 f his best from this period\, Drunken Angel has been compared to Billy Wil
 der’s The Lost Weekend (1945)\, as both interrogate the consequences of 
 decisions clouded by excessive drink.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/drunken-angel-yoidore
 -tenshi-in-35-mm-019c0633-db8a-4cd0-8734-8871f33ced20
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:No Regrets for Our Youth (Waga seishun ni kui nashi) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260409T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260409T203000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0634-6642-ca6a-7f2a-3129b42e2524@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, April 9\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Muse
 um of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 50m\n\n1946 | 110 min. | Japan | Blac
 k-and-White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTE
 D BY: Akira Kurosawa  \nWRITTEN BY: Eijirô Hisaita  \nWITH: Setsuko Har
 a\, Denjiro Okochi\, Eiko Miyoshi\, Susumu Fujita\nPrint courtesy of The J
 apan Foundation\, Los Angeles.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nFollowing the Allied
  occupation of Japan\, and in order to avoid censorship\, Kurosawa’s fil
 ms began to reflect the newly embraced pro-democracy worldview sweeping th
 e country. These restrictions on story and theme led to the creation of on
 e of his few female protagonists\, the complex yet relatable Yukie Yagihar
 a\, embodied by celebrated actress and frequent future Ozu collaborator Se
 tsuko Hara (Late Spring\, 1949). Beginning in the aftermath of the Mukden 
 incident\, which incited the Japanese invasion of Manchuria\, and jumping 
 forward through the 1930s\, Yukie navigates love\, livelihood\, family tie
 s\, and her future in a film that the director later revealed is deeply no
 stalgic for his own years spent coming of age before World War II. No Regr
 ets for Our Youth was released the same year as Those Who Make Tomorrow\, 
 the only feature-length film directed by Kurosawa considered to be lost.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/no-regrets-for-our-yo
 uth-waga-seishun-ni-kui-nashi-in-35-mm-019c0634-6642-ca6a-7f2a-3129b42e252
 4
END:VEVENT
