BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Stray Dog (Nora inu) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260410T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260410T203000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0634-e877-2a82-3436-1d52e10104d4@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, April 10\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 2m\n\n1949 | 122 min. | Japan | Black-
 and-White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED 
 BY: Akira Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Ryuzo Kikushima\, Akira Kurosawa \nWITH: 
 Toshiro Mifune\, Takashi Shimura\, Keiko Awaji\, Eiko Miyoshi\n\nType: Scr
 eenings\n\nRookie detective Murakami (Toshiro Mifune) loses his pistol to 
 a pickpocket in a crowded\, sweltering streetcar during his first day on t
 he job. To save face\, and with the help of skilled detective Sato (Takash
 i Shimura)\, he goes undercover through Tokyo’s underground to capture t
 he culprit before any crimes are committed. An early breakthrough in Kuros
 awa’s career\, Stray Dog explores the desperation of postwar Japanese so
 ciety. Isao Kimura’s portrayal of the disillusioned war veteran-turned-t
 hief marked the actor’s first appearance in a Kurosawa film.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/stray-dog-in-35-mm-01
 9c0634-e877-2a82-3436-1d52e10104d4
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Spirited Away
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260411T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260411T121500
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c3013-b55b-e00d-b452-9223ae6256ac@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 11\, 2026 at 11 am\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 5m\n\n2001 | 125 min. | Japan | Color 
 | English (Dubbed) | Rated PG | DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Hayao Miyazaki\n
 WITH: Daveigh Chase\, Suzanne Pleshette\, Jason Marsden\, Susan Egan\n\nTy
 pe: Screenings\n\nSpirited Away was Hayao Miyazaki’s first Oscar win\, a
 nd the first hand-drawn non-English-language animated film to win the Acad
 emy Award for Best Animated Feature in 2003. The film invites audiences in
 to a mind-bending supernatural realm when a wrong turn on a family road tr
 ip lands 10-year-old Chihiro in a mysterious dimension populated by all ma
 nner of creatures\, including soot gremlins (first introduced in My Neighb
 or Totoro)\, the giant witch Yubaba\, and\, most memorably\, the enigmatic
  No Face. To find her parents and return to the real world\, Chihiro must 
 work at an enchanted bathhouse serving spirits of all shapes and sizes. A 
 tour de force of surrealist invention\, Spirited Away is a timeless wonder
  filled with whimsy and joy.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/spirited-away-019c301
 3-b55b-e00d-b452-9223ae6256ac
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Most Beautiful (Ichiban utsukushiku) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260411T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260411T160000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0639-581f-93f7-96c6-4d5b0309850e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 11\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mus
 eum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 24m\n\n1944 | 84 min. | Japan | Blac
 k-and-White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED
 /WRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa  \nWITH: Takashi Shimura\, Soji Kiyokawa\, Ic
 hiro Sugai\, Takako Irie\nPrint courtesy of The Japan Foundation\, Los Ang
 eles.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nKurosawa’s second feature film takes a docu
 mentary-like approach to the fictionalized story of young women working in
  a wartime optics factory. Featuring a cast of dozens and shot on location
  at the Nippon Kogaku factory\, The Most Beautiful experiments with narrat
 ive and exhibits Kurosawa’s skill with depth-of-field cinematography wit
 hin his creative use of mise-en-scène. Tinged with the visual language an
 d conceit of a Soviet-era propaganda film and steeped in wartime patriotis
 m—especially in scenes of group activities such as volleyball matches an
 d band practice—this portrait of female camaraderie stands out among Kur
 osawa’s oeuvre for its distinct focus on women protagonists and for pred
 ating the social realist filmmaking that would emerge in postwar Japan.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-most-beautiful-ic
 hiban-utsukushiku-in-35-mm-019c0639-581f-93f7-96c6-4d5b0309850e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Zodiac with David Fincher and Bong Joon Ho
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260411T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260411T223000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c7ce1-27ac-6b1c-34b6-7442623ce54d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 11\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mus
 eum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 37m\n\n2007 | 157 min. | USA | Engli
 sh | Color | Rated R | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: David Fincher \nWRITTEN BY: Jame
 s Vanderbilt \nWITH: Jake Gyllenhaal\, Robert Downey Jr.\, Mark Ruffalo\, 
 Anthony Edwards\n\nType: Screenings\n\nDirector David Fincher’s 2007 thr
 iller excavates a notorious series of real unsolved murders that petrified
  the San Francisco Bay Area in the late 1960s. Jake Gyllenhaal portrays Ro
 bert Graysmith\, a cartoonist whose amateur investigation into the serial 
 killer\, known only as Zodiac\, complements the efforts of SFPD inspector 
 Dave Toschi (Mark Ruffalo) and San Francisco Chronicle reporter Paul Avery
  (Robert Downey Jr.). \nFincher’s dense narrative offers various interpr
 etations of the crimes and Zodiac’s identity\, avoiding the cliché chan
 nelings typical of crime thrillers. An original poster from the film is on
  view in the current exhibition Director’s Inspiration: Bong Joon Ho.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/zodiac-019c7ce1-27ac-
 6b1c-34b6-7442623ce54d
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Alfie in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260412T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260412T160000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be225-9862-2630-f28b-ee6709e16f68@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 12\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum o
 f Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 54m\n\n1966 | 114 min. | UK | Color | Sco
 pe | English | Rated PG | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Lewis Gilbert \nWRITTEN BY: 
 Bill Naughton \nWITH: Michael Caine\, Millicent Martin\, Vivien Merchant\,
  Shelley Winters \nNew print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType
 : Screenings\n\nMichael Caine achieved international stardom and received 
 his first Best Actor nomination for his wry performance as a charismatic l
 othario in this comedy-drama from producer-director Lewis Gilbert (You Onl
 y Live Twice\, 1967) that is a lively time capsule of Swinging London. Gil
 bert and casting supervisor Sally Nicholl supported Caine with an impressi
 ve array of actresses\, including two-time Oscar winner Shelley Winters\, 
 as well as Jane Asher\, Shirley Anne Field\, and stage actress Vivien Merc
 hant in a touching\, nominated performance as one of Alfie’s married con
 quests. \nThe film was also nominated for Best Picture (Gilbert)\, Bill Na
 ughton’s adaptation of his own play\, and the classic title song by Burt
  Bacharach and Hal David\, performed for the film’s end credits by Cher.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/alfie-019be225-9862-2
 630-f28b-ee6709e16f68
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Men Who Tread on the Tiger’s Tail (Tora no o o wo fumu otoko
 tachi) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260412T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260412T160000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0639-c712-81a0-657e-c7b4f489e043@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 12\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 59m\n\n1945/1952 | 59 min. | Japan | Blac
 k-and-White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED
 /WRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\nWITH: Denjiro Ookouchi\, Takashi Shimura\, Su
 sumu Fujita\, Masayuki Mori\nPrint courtesy of The Japan Foundation\, Los 
 Angeles.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nShot exclusively on the soundstages of Toh
 o studios\, Kurosawa’s fourth feature is based on a kabuki play that dep
 icts a 12th-century tale of Yoshitsune\, a renowned commander of the Minam
 oto clan\, and six samurai subordinates who disguise themselves as monks t
 o cross enemy territory. Banned in postwar Japan by the US occupation for 
 depicting feudal loyalties\, this historical drama was finally screened in
  Japan after the Treaty of San Francisco signaled the withdrawal of US for
 ces in 1952. The story is cinematically aligned with rhythmic dialogues in
 cluding chanting and singing executed by the incredibly talented ensemble.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-men-who-tread-on-
 the-tigers-tail-tora-no-o-o-wo-fumu-otokotachi-in-35-mm-019c0639-c712-81a0
 -657e-c7b4f489e043
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Quiet Duel (Shizukanaru ketto) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260412T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260412T203000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c063c-9531-b10b-a6c4-48e5b6611ae3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 12\, 2026 at 6 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum o
 f Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1949 | 95 min. | Japan | Black-and
 -White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: 
 Akira Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Kazuo Kikuta\, Senkichi Tani
 guchi \nWITH: Toshiro Mifune\, Takashi Shimura\, Miki Sanjo\, Kenjiro Uem
 ura\nPrint courtesy of The Japan Foundation\, Los Angeles.\n\nType: Screen
 ings\n\nDuring an urgent medical operation\, a young doctor\, Kyoji Fujisa
 ki\, accidentally cuts himself and contracts syphilis\, which puts constra
 ints on his life and personal relationships\, especially with his fiancée
 . Toshiro Mifune poignantly explores Fujisaki’s agony and self-imposed i
 solation while he battles conflicting emotions of morality and desire. Kur
 osawa enhances the film with shots and depth of field that are meticulousl
 y timed and framed\, and he creates a claustrophobic atmosphere by setting
  the action almost exclusively within the shabby confines of Fujisaki’s 
 hospital.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-quiet-duel-in-35-
 mm-019c063c-9531-b10b-a6c4-48e5b6611ae3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Mickey 17 with Bong Joon Ho
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260412T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260412T223000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c7ce2-1b21-9c94-fd1a-1ddbbf2ede6b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 12\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 17m\n\n2025 | 137 min. | USA | English
  | Color | Rated R | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Bong Joon Ho \nWITH: Ro
 bert Pattinson\, Naomi Ackie\, Mark Ruffalo\, Toni Collette\, Steven Yeun\
 n\nType: Screenings\n\nBong Joon Ho’s quirky sci-fi comedy\, based on Ed
 ward Ashton’s novel Mickey7 (2022)\, showcases a star ensemble and lavis
 h production design. The film follows the naive Mickey Barnes (Robert Patt
 ison)\, an “Expendable” who volunteers for work that is often deadly. 
 After every fatality\, the project leaders\, an ambitious politician and h
 is wife\, Kenneth and Ylfa (Mark Ruffalo and Toni Collette)\, simply print
  out a new Mickey and carry on—until they make a fateful mistake one day
 . Stunning art direction and crisp cinematography elevate an intriguing st
 ory marked by Bong’s signature artistry—masterful execution of continu
 ous tonal shifts and genre bending—as he underscores the sociopolitical 
 issues often found in his work.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/mickey-17-019c7ce2-1b
 21-9c94-fd1a-1ddbbf2ede6b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Rocco and His Brothers in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260413T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260413T163000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be238-35a0-48e3-d51e-df9491fbee54@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, April 13\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 59m\n\n1960 | 179 min. | Italy/France 
 | Black-and-White | Italian with English subtitles | Not Rated | 4K DCP  \
 nDIRECTED BY: Luchino Visconti  \nWRITTEN BY: Suso Cecchi D’Amico\, Pas
 quale Festa Campanile\, Massimo Franciosa\, Enrico Medioli\, Luchino Visco
 nti  \nSTORY BY: Luchino Visconti\, Suso Cecchi D’Amico\, Vasco Pratoli
 ni  \nWITH: Alain Delon\, Renato Salvatori\, Annie Girardot\, Katina Paxi
 nou\n\nType: Screenings\n\nRocco and His Brothers was a later discovery fo
 r Bong Joon Ho\, who put the film on his 2022 Sight and Sound list as one 
 of the 10 films he considers the greatest of all time. An epic melodrama f
 rom director Luchino Visconti\, the film helped to make an international s
 tar of French actor Alain Delon\, cast as one of five sons of a poor rural
  Italian family whose fortunes fall even further after a move to Milan. Th
 e screenplay was a collaboration between Visconti and four other writers\,
  including Suso Cecchi D’Amico—one of the first female screenwriters i
 n Italian cinema and a key figure in the neorealist movement\; her body of
  work includes such classics as Bicycle Thieves (1948).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/rocco-and-his-brother
 s-019be238-35a0-48e3-d51e-df9491fbee54
END:VEVENT
