SUMMARY:The Bad Sleep Well (Warui yatsu hodo yoku nemuru) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260425T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260425T203000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c064d-95c8-0bd5-dbac-becff4634f3f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 25\, 2026 at 7 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 31m\n\n1960 | 151 min. | Japan | Black-
 and-White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED B
 Y: Akira Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Hideo Oguni\, Eijirô Hisaita\, Akira Kur
 osawa\, Ryûkô Kikushima\, Shinobu Hashimoto  \nWITH: Toshiro Mifune\, M
 asayuki Mori\, Kyoko Kagawa\, Tatsuya Mihashi\n\nType: Screenings\n\nBy 19
 60\, Kurosawa had been in the film industry for half his life\, excelling 
 with contemporary stories (gendai-geki) and period pieces (jidai-geki) ali
 ke for Toho Studios\, Daiei Film\, and Shochiku Co. For The Bad Sleep Well
 \, the filmmaker founded Kurosawa Films\, knowing that the daring stylisti
 c choices and topical themes he wanted to explore would cause friction wit
 h the majors. A neo-noir inspired by Shakespeare’s Hamlet\, set in the c
 orporate boardrooms of postwar Tokyo\, Kurosawa’s nineteenth feature aga
 in casts the singular Toshiro Mifune at the center of a twisted cover-up t
 hat uncovers the sticky topics of corruption\, shame\, and greed.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-bad-sleep-well-wa
 rui-yatsu-hodo-yoku-nemuru-in-35-mm-019c064d-95c8-0bd5-dbac-becff4634f3f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ordinary People in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260426T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260426T160000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be227-18dd-4cba-6348-0875e69008cd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 26\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum o
 f Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 4m\n\n1980 | 124 min. | USA | Color | Eng
 lish | Rated R | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Robert Redford \nWRITTEN BY: Alvin Sa
 rgent \nWITH: Donald Sutherland\, Mary Tyler Moore\, Judd Hirsch\, Timothy
  Hutton \nNew print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screeni
 ngs\n\nRobert Redford made his feature directorial debut with this moving 
 adaptation of Judith Guest’s novel about the difficult relationship betw
 een an emotionally troubled teenager and his parents after the death of hi
 s brother. The cast\, selected by Redford and casting director Penny Perry
 \, balanced veteran actors such as Donald Sutherland\, Mary Tyler Moore (e
 arning a Best Actress nomination for a rare dramatic role)\, and Supportin
 g Actor nominee Judd Hirsch with relative newcomers such as Elizabeth McGo
 vern\, Dinah Manoff\, and Timothy Hutton\, who won Best Supporting Actor f
 or his sensitive performance as the guilt-ridden Conrad. The film earned a
 dditional Oscars for Best Picture (Ronald L. Schwary\, producer)\, Redford
 ’s direction\, and Alvin Sargent’s screenplay.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/ordinary-people-019be
 227-18dd-4cba-6348-0875e69008cd
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Janie Geiser’s Time\, a substance
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260426T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260426T203000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be21a-5319-b8b1-8b4b-c13615afb76e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: filmmaker Janie Geiser in conversation with USC pro
 fessor Holly Willis\n\nSunday\, April 26\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Ac
 ademy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 8m\n\nReverse Shadow \n2019. 
 8 min. USA. Color. Sound. Digital  \nAbsent Objects \n2020. 8 min. USA. C
 olor. Sound. Digital \n22 Light-Years \n2021. 18 min. USA. Color. Sound. D
 igital \nVaporetto  \n2021. 3 min. USA. Color. Sound. Digital \nHeliotrop
 e \n2023. 7 min. USA. Color. Sound. Digital  \nChameleon Law \n2022. 8 mi
 n. USA. Color. Sound. Digital \nSudden Tourniquet \n2025. 8 min. USA. Col
 or. Digital \nTotal program runtime: approx. 60 min.\n\nType: Screenings\n
 \nSouthern California-based artist Janie Geiser’s wide-ranging practice 
 includes performance\, film\, installation\, and art. Known for its recont
 extualization of abandoned images and objects\; embrace of artifice\; and 
 investigation of memory\, power\, and loss\, Geiser’s work “prowls aro
 und the mysterious boundary-land between territories—like the lands of t
 he conscious and the unconscious mind\, or life and death” (Holly Willis
 ). The seven recent works that encompass Time\, a substance span 2019–25
 \, and include several that were made during the early years of the pandem
 ic. The films move in and out of tangible and intangible spaces\, defined 
 by collage sources including faded photographic slides\, empty photo album
 s\, a child’s shooting game\, photographs\, negatives\, and renderings c
 reated with a desktop home designer program. Each film’s distinct visual
  and sonic texture translates to a uniquely haptic experience\, with Geise
 r’s layering and close-ups striking a deep chord in our sense memories. 
  \nAll films directed by Janie Geiser.  \nProgrammed by Hyesung ii and K
 .J. Relth-Miller
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/janie-geisers-time-a-
 substance-019be21a-5319-b8b1-8b4b-c13615afb76e
END:VEVENT
