BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Robin and Marian in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260516T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260516T160000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0657-fdeb-4772-f25d-e687eb4bf90e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 16\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 46m\n\n1976 | 106 min. | USA | Color |
  English | Rated PG | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Richard Lester \nWRITTEN: Jame
 s Goldman \nWITH: Sean Connery\, Audrey Hepburn\, Robert Shaw\, Richard Ha
 rris\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAudrey Hepburn made a welcome return to the bi
 g screen after a nine-year absence in a touching performance as Maid Maria
 n in this romantic swashbuckler with Sean Connery (at his most vulnerable)
  as Robin Hood. \nRichard Lester (The Three Musketeers\, 1973) directed th
 e tragicomic original screenplay by Oscar winner James Goldman (The Lion i
 n Winter\, 1968)\, and the supporting cast features an astonishing assembl
 age of British acting talent including Richard Harris\, Denholm Elliott\, 
 Nicol Williamson\, Ian Holm\, and Robert Shaw in a quietly powerful perfor
 mance as Robin’s perennial nemesis\, the Sheriff of Nottingham.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/robin-and-marian-in-4
 -k-019c0657-fdeb-4772-f25d-e687eb4bf90e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Odd Man Out
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260516T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260516T203000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0658-767a-4e1d-1109-50eca3c0581d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 16\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 56m\n\n1947 | 116 min. | UK | Black-an
 d-White | English | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Carol Reed \nWRITTEN B
 Y: F. L. Green\, R. C. Sherriff \nWITH: James Mason\, Robert Newton\, Cyr
 il Cusack\, F. J. McCormick\n\nType: Screenings\n\nWounded IRA fugitive Jo
 hnny McQueen (James Mason) flees through wintry Belfast\, encountering str
 angers while reuniting with his lover. Over 24 hours\, the exhausted McQue
 en displays the growing desperation of man with nowhere left to turn. \nSh
 ooting on the streets of West Belfast in Northern Ireland and in Hackney\,
  London\, filmmaker Carol Reed employs the evocative locations and stark c
 inematography that would come to define his later film The Third Man (1949
 ). Few directors in the 1940s matched Reed’s fatalistic intensity\, tran
 sforming mundane details into evocations of an unbalanced world.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/odd-man-out-019c0658-
 767a-4e1d-1109-50eca3c0581d
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Secrets & Lies
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T160000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be228-4c81-ee7e-c1b4-a971b99149cf@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 17\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of 
 Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 16m\n\n1996 | 136 min. | UK | Color | Engli
 sh | Rated R | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Mike Leigh \nWITH: Brenda Blethy
 n\, Marianne Jean-Baptiste\, Timothy Spall\, Claire Rushbrook\n\nType: Scr
 eenings\n\nA woman is united with the adult daughter she never knew in thi
 s original and authentic comedy-drama from seven-time Oscar nominated writ
 er-director Mike Leigh. Leigh created the script by his usual method\, wor
 king with his top team of actors (assembled by casting directors Paddy Ste
 rn and Susie Parriss) to develop the characters and storyline\, and the re
 sult earned nominations for stars Brenda Blethyn and Marianne Jean-Baptist
 e as the unlikely mother-daughter pair. \nThe cast includes Leigh regulars
  such as Timothy Spall and Lesley Manville\, and the film was also nominat
 ed for Best Picture (Simon Channing-Williams\, producer)\, Leigh’s direc
 tion\, and the film’s original screenplay.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/secrets-lies-019be228
 -4c81-ee7e-c1b4-a971b99149cf
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Black Sunday in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T160000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0659-003c-33c2-6b9a-8b303cabef76@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 17\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 23m\n\n1977 | 143 min. | USA | Color | S
 cope | English | Rated R | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: John Frankenheimer \nWRITTE
 N BY: Ernest Lehman\, Kenneth Ross\, Ivan Moffat \nWITH: Robert Shaw\, Bru
 ce Dern\, Marthe Keller\, Bekim Fehmiu\n\nType: Screenings\n\nJohn Franken
 heimer (The Manchurian Candidate\, 1962) directed this epic international 
 thriller based on the debut novel by Thomas Harris\, creator of Hannibal L
 ecter. Robert Shaw\, in a rare and welcome heroic leading role\, is a worl
 d-weary Mossad agent on the trail of a Black September terrorist (Marthe K
 eller) and her unstable partner (Bruce Dern) planning an unthinkable crime
  on US soil.\nJohn Williams’s score adds to the pulse-pounding excitemen
 t\, and the screenplay by Ernest Lehman\, Kenneth Ross\, and Ivan Moffat g
 ives the characters on both sides of the conflict their due.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/black-sunday-in-35-mm
 -019c0659-003c-33c2-6b9a-8b303cabef76
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Pool of London
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T203000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c0659-a2fd-1d33-5589-96ff6eeda5fd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 17\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 25m\n\n1951 | 85 min. | UK | Black-and-W
 hite | English | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Basil Dearden  \nWRITTEN 
 BY: Jack Whittingham\, John Eldridge  \nWITH: Bonar Colleano\, Susan Shaw
 \, Earl Cameron\, Renée Asherson\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThis remarkable L
 ondon-set crime drama tells the story of Dan MacDonald (Bonar Colleano) an
 d Johnny Lambert (Earl Cameron)\, two seamen entangled in a smuggling rack
 et. Filmmaker Basil Dearden\, one of Britain’s finest talents\, directs 
 with his typical rigor and makes a bold choice for its time by casting Bla
 ck actor Cameron as the romantic lead. Dearden lends an eerie despair to L
 ondon’s winding streets\, famous bridges\, dark tunnels\, and massive ca
 thedrals by virtually emptying them of other people\, creating a sense of 
 isolation that echoes the region’s prevailing postwar sentiments.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/pool-of-london-019c06
 59-a2fd-1d33-5589-96ff6eeda5fd
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Barton Fink in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260518T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260518T163000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019be23e-fa7c-bce5-3e19-60258b8caa53@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, May 18\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 56m\n\n1991 | 116 min. | UK/USA | Color 
 | English | Rated R | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Joel Coen  \nWRITTEN BY: Joel Co
 en\, Ethan Coen  \nWITH: John Turturro\, John Goodman\, Judy Davis\, Mich
 ael Lerner  \nPrint courtesy of the Ed and Maya Herrera collection at the
  Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\n“I’ve always been drawn 
 to the Coen Brothers\,” Bong Joon Ho says in the catalog for the exhibit
 ion Director’s Inspiration: Bong Joon Ho. “Their movies are full of mi
 sunderstandings and misinterpretations.” Set in 1941\, Barton Fink is th
 e story of the titular East Coast transplant (played by John Turturro) who
  abandons his playwrighting career to try his hand in Hollywood. During a 
 bout of writer’s block in his dilapidated hotel\, Fink becomes acquainte
 d with his noisy neighbor (John Goodman)\, an insurance salesman whose aff
 able appearance belies his true nature. The fourth feature by the Coens wa
 s also the first to win all three major prizes—Best Director\, Best Acto
 r\, and the Palme d’Or—at the Cannes Film Festival. The film was nomin
 ated at the 64th Academy Awards for Supporting Actor (Michael Lerner)\, Ar
 t Direction\, and Costume Design.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/barton-fink-019be23e-
 fa7c-bce5-3e19-60258b8caa53
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Passionate Friends
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260518T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260518T203000
DTSTAMP:20260401T083316Z
UID:019c065b-931a-f68a-6dc8-35c6665ebbf8@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, May 18\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1949 | 95 min. | UK | Black-and-W
 hite | English | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: David Lean\nWRITTEN BY: Er
 ic Ambler\, David Lean\, Stanley Haynes\nWITH: Ann Todd\, Claude Rains\, T
 revor Howard\, Betty Ann Davies\n\nType: Screenings\n\nBased on H. G. Well
 s’s story of former lovers who rekindle their romance in Switzerland\, t
 his is one of David Lean’s most passionate love stories\, elevated by Gu
 y Green’s lyrical location cinematography in London\, Switzerland\, and 
 France. Mary Justin (Ann Todd) is married to her jealous husband\, Howard 
 (Claude Rains)\, but finds herself besotted with old flame Steven Stratton
  (Trevor Howard). A continuation of the themes explored in the devastating
  Brief Encounter (1945)\, the film embraces a female perspective\, allowin
 g the viewer to hear Mary’s interior monologue as she navigates the pass
 ion she wants and stability she needs.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-passionate-friend
 s-019c065b-931a-f68a-6dc8-35c6665ebbf8
END:VEVENT
